E AX3050 result-2.ax:1:17-23 contract-malformed "`result` names nothing here: this declaration has no `::` signature, so it declares no result type for `result` to have" ?"a `;@axiom:pre(...)` or `;@axiom:post(...)` is a claim the COMPILER cannot decide - there is no value analysis here - so it is compiled into the body and checked at run time, on every call, and a failure writes `axiom: precondition failed in `f`: ...` on fd 2 and exits 80. That is why the expression has to parse, has to be `Bool`, and has to PERFORM nothing: a contract that allocates or writes changes the program by being stated. Comparison, indexing, `strEq`, `strLen`, `vecLen` and `vecGet` are allocation-free and are what a predicate is written from; `concat`, `fmtInt` and `vecNew` are not. `result` names the declared result of a `post` and nothing anywhere else"
E AX3050 result-3.ax:1:16-22 contract-malformed "`result` names nothing in a `pre`: a precondition is checked BEFORE the body runs, so there is no result yet" ?"a `;@axiom:pre(...)` or `;@axiom:post(...)` is a claim the COMPILER cannot decide - there is no value analysis here - so it is compiled into the body and checked at run time, on every call, and a failure writes `axiom: precondition failed in `f`: ...` on fd 2 and exits 80. That is why the expression has to parse, has to be `Bool`, and has to PERFORM nothing: a contract that allocates or writes changes the program by being stated. Comparison, indexing, `strEq`, `strLen`, `vecLen` and `vecGet` are allocation-free and are what a predicate is written from; `concat`, `fmtInt` and `vecNew` are not. `result` names the declared result of a `post` and nothing anywhere else"
